{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And they journeyed from the mountain of HaShem.</b> Israel journeyed from Mount Sinai and the cloud was before them, and the Ark went after the cloud. The cloud did not rest in any place until the third day at evening, for it sought a good place for them to encamp — this is the sense of ״to seek out a resting place for them.״ And the Sages of blessed memory interpreted: ״from the mountain of HaShem״ — מאחרי ה׳, 'away from HaShem,' that is to say that they lusted with craving. This was the twenty-second of Iyyar, for on the twentieth of the second month the cloud was taken up, and the first day of the three days mentioned here in the phrase ״a journey of three days״ is the twentieth itself; if so, on the twenty-second of Iyyar they lusted with craving and ate flesh until the twenty-second of Sivan. And the reason they needed to interpret thus is that they observed that nowhere in the Torah is found except ״the mountain of Elohim״ (Exodus 24:13) — ״and Moses went up onto the mountain of Elohim״ — and likewise (Exodus [3:1]) ״unto the mountain of Elohim, unto Horeb,״ and likewise it is found in the Masorah that there are only two instances in the Torah (Genesis 22:14): ״in the mount of HaShem it shall be seen״ and ״and they journeyed from the mount of HaShem.״ And since the expression changed and it said ״the mount of HaShem,״ they needed to interpret that they journeyed away from HaShem."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:05.973659744Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}